@chapter 6502 Backend
@cindex 6502 backend

The 6502 backend generates assembly code for the MOS Technology 6502 8-bit microprocessor
and its successors. The 6502 is one of the most widely-used processors in retro computing,
found in systems including the Commodore 64, Apple II, NES, and many arcade machines.

@section Architecture Overview

The 6502 is an 8-bit processor with:
@itemize
@item A single 8-bit accumulator (A)
@item Two 8-bit index registers (X, Y)
@item An 8-bit status register (flags)
@item 16-bit address bus (64KB address space)
@item 256-byte zero page (fast memory area)
@item 256-byte hardware stack (page $01)
@end itemize

@section Register Allocation

The EIGHTBOL 6502 backend uses the following register allocation strategy:

@table @asis
@item A (Accumulator)
Primary register for arithmetic and logic operations. Used for function return values.

@item X (Index Register)
Typically used for loop counters and array indexing.

@item Y (Index Register)
Typically used for secondary indexing or temporary storage.

@item Zero Page ($00-$FF)
Used for local variables and temporary storage. The compiler allocates space dynamically.

@item Stack ($0100-$01FF)
Used for call returns and local stack frames.
@end table

@section Function Call Convention

@subsection Subroutine Calls

Subroutine calls use the JSR (Jump to Subroutine) instruction:

@example
JSR subroutine-name    ; Push return address and jump
RTS                    ; Return to caller
@end example

The 6502 automatically pushes a 16-bit return address (minus 1) onto the stack.

@subsection Return Values

Return values are passed in the accumulator (A) for single-byte results. 
Multi-byte results are returned via zero-page locations or stack conventions.

@subsection Parameter Passing

Parameters are passed via:
@itemize
@item Zero-page locations for small parameters
@item The accumulator for primary parameters
@item The stack for large or multiple parameters
@end itemize

@section Address Modes

The 6502 supports multiple addressing modes:

@table @asis
@item Immediate
@code{#$FF} - Load constant value directly

@item Zero Page
@code{$80} - Access single byte in zero page

@item Zero Page, X
@code{$80,X} - Zero page with X offset

@item Zero Page, Y
@code{$80,Y} - Zero page with Y offset

@item Absolute
@code{$1234} - 16-bit address

@item Absolute, X
@code{$1234,X} - Absolute with X offset

@item Absolute, Y
@code{$1234,Y} - Absolute with Y offset

@item Indirect
@code{($1234)} - Indirect addressing (JMP only)

@item Indexed Indirect
@code{($80,X)} - Indirect with X offset

@item Indirect Indexed
@code{($80),Y} - Indirect with Y offset (post-indexed)

@item Relative
@code{+/- offset} - Branch instructions
@end table

@section Instruction Set Summary

@subsection Load/Store Instructions

@table @asis
@item LDA
Load Accumulator (affects N, Z flags)

@item LDX
Load X Register (affects N, Z flags)

@item LDY
Load Y Register (affects N, Z flags)

@item STA
Store Accumulator

@item STX
Store X Register

@item STY
Store Y Register
@end table

@subsection Arithmetic Instructions

@table @asis
@item ADC
Add with Carry (accumulator + memory + carry flag)

@item SBC
Subtract with Carry (accumulator - memory - not carry flag)

@item CMP
Compare (A - memory, affects N, Z, C flags)

@item CPX
Compare X (X - memory, affects N, Z, C flags)

@item CPY
Compare Y (Y - memory, affects N, Z, C flags)
@end table

@subsection Logical Instructions

@table @asis
@item AND
Bitwise AND (affects N, Z flags)

@item ORA
Bitwise OR (affects N, Z flags)

@item EOR
Bitwise XOR (affects N, Z flags)

@item BIT
Bit test (affects N, V, Z flags)
@end table

@subsection Shift/Rotate Instructions

@table @asis
@item ASL
Arithmetic Shift Left (multiply by 2)

@item LSR
Logical Shift Right (divide by 2 unsigned)

@item ROL
Rotate Left through Carry

@item ROR
Rotate Right through Carry
@end table

@subsection Jump/Branch Instructions

@table @asis
@item JMP
Unconditional Jump

@item JSR
Jump to Subroutine (push return address)

@item RTS
Return from Subroutine (pop return address)

@item BEQ
Branch if Equal (Z flag set)

@item BNE
Branch if Not Equal (Z flag clear)

@item BMI
Branch if Minus (N flag set)

@item BPL
Branch if Plus (N flag clear)

@item BVC
Branch if Overflow Clear (V flag clear)

@item BVS
Branch if Overflow Set (V flag set)

@item BCC
Branch if Carry Clear (C flag clear)

@item BCS
Branch if Carry Set (C flag set)
@end table

@subsection Increment/Decrement Instructions

@table @asis
@item INC
Increment (affects N, Z flags)

@item DEC
Decrement (affects N, Z flags)

@item INX
Increment X (affects N, Z flags)

@item INY
Increment Y (affects N, Z flags)

@item DEX
Decrement X (affects N, Z flags)

@item DEY
Decrement Y (affects N, Z flags)
@end table

@subsection Stack Instructions

@table @asis
@item PHA
Push Accumulator

@item PLA
Pull (Pop) Accumulator (affects N, Z flags)

@item PHP
Push Processor Status (flags)

@item PLP
Pull (Pop) Processor Status

@item TXS
Transfer X to Stack Pointer

@item TSX
Transfer Stack Pointer to X
@end table

@section Status Flags

The 6502 status register contains 8 flags:

@table @asis
@item N (Negative)
Set if result has bit 7 set (is negative in signed representation)

@item V (Overflow)
Set if arithmetic operation resulted in signed overflow

@item - (unused)
Bit 5 is unused

@item B (Break)
Set when BRK instruction executed

@item D (Decimal)
Set to enable decimal (BCD) arithmetic mode

@item I (Interrupt)
Set to disable interrupts

@item Z (Zero)
Set if result is zero

@item C (Carry)
Set if arithmetic operation produced carry or borrow
@end table

@section Numeric Precision

The 6502 backend supports:
@itemize
@item 8-bit unsigned (0-255)
@item 8-bit signed (-128 to 127)
@item 16-bit unsigned (0-65535) via multi-byte operations
@item 16-bit signed (-32768 to 32767) via multi-byte operations
@item Binary fixed-point with arbitrary precision
@item BCD (Binary Coded Decimal) via decimal mode
@end itemize

@section Code Generation Strategy

@subsection Variable Storage

Local variables are allocated in zero page when possible (fast access).
For larger programs, stack-based allocation is used.

@subsection Expression Evaluation

Expressions are typically evaluated left-to-right with results accumulated in:
@itemize
@item The A register (primary result)
@item Zero-page locations (for intermediate results)
@item The stack (for multi-value operations)
@end itemize

@subsection Loop Generation

Counted loops typically use X or Y register as counter:

@example
LDX #$0A        ; Load counter (10)
LoopLabel:
  ; Loop body here
  DEX             ; Decrement counter
  BNE LoopLabel   ; Branch if not equal to zero
@end example

@subsection Conditional Execution

Conditions are evaluated and flags are tested via branch instructions:

@example
; IF condition THEN...
CMP #0          ; Compare A with zero
BEQ ThenLabel   ; Branch if equal (true)
; ELSE clause here
JMP ElseEnd
ThenLabel:
; THEN clause here
ElseEnd:
@end example

@section Optimization Strategies

@itemize
@item Zero-page addressing for hot variables
@item Register caching to minimize loads/stores
@item Dead code elimination
@item Branch target optimization
@item Constant propagation
@end itemize

@section Platform Specifics

Different 6502-based systems have different memory layouts and conventions.
The EIGHTBOL compiler can target:

@subsection Commodore 64
- 64KB address space
- Zero page: $00-$FF
- Stack: $0100-$01FF
- BASIC ROM: $A000-$BFFF
- Kernal ROM: $E000-$FFFF

@subsection Apple II
- 64KB address space (48KB standard)
- Zero page: $00-$FF
- Stack: $0100-$01FF
- Alternate ROM banks

@subsection NES (RP2A03)
See backend-rp2a03 for NES-specific details

@subsection Atari 2600 (6507)
Derived from 6502 with reduced address space and I/O
